ery common  occurrence in steel bull run because  most antagonists and other incidental  characters are well defined and  humanized which gives us world over  sudden so much more depth of feel  something that I have not experienced in  Prior parts and I have to praise are key  for finally finding that sweet sweet  balance and for being bold enough to  take the time to write and draw  flashbacks for these characters that  allow us to emot-- with them for a  change and I know that he does do this  selectively for other characters in  previous parts but Stardust Crusaders  diamond is unbreakable golden wind 'red  stone ocean all felt like they featured  a cast of fairly flat stand users  outside of our main group although to be  fair I should give golden wind more  credit than that because I feel like arc  you really did try to flush out the  gaggle of stand users there but steel  ball run is the real deal  and now I refuse to go any further  without talking about our main character  gyro zeppeli which we know because the  manga literally says at one point o main  character gyro zeppeli but I guess he's  technically more of a deuteragonist but  at the same time this incredibly  compelling figure is the driving force  behind so much of this series that I'm  just going to consider him the  protagonist and I love pretty much  everything about gyro his design is  fantastic his story is deep and  empathetic and he also has a nice unique  fighting style via the steel balls which  was a very nice surprise because at this  point I'm very much used to a giorgo  universe where a character can't do  anything whatsoever without a stand  power and while he does eventually  manifest what I think is a stand right  at the climax of this  he spends the majority of his time  facing off against these insane beings  with nothing but a pair of balls  Plus also he decided to take his teddy  with him during the race so what is not  to like about this guy meanwhile al Jojo  for the spot Jonny is also a nice breath  of fresh air in many many ways firstly a  paraplegic jojo is a pretty damn big  deal because he starts the series behind  every other Jojo many of whom were  godlike figures both physically and  mentally from the get-go but Jonny well  he has some serious faults in both of  those areas and that is fantastic  because that is what going on a journey  is all about facing these faults  overcoming them and evolving which Jonny  is in a consistent process of doing all  the way up to the end of steel ball run  and I guess most importantly Jonny  always feels like an underdog especially  with his initially underwhelming stand  ability which is so far removed from the  insanity of stuff like stoned free or  gold experience although through the  focus on rotation Jonny does of course  turn it into an absurdly formidable  power Jonny also gave me pause for  thought though on a number of occasions  because he isn't a simple case of you  know a quote-unquote good guy he is a  very almost morally gray kind of dude  bruh because yes he had an admittedly  tragic childhood and then went through a  phase of being a scumbag of the highest  order but even as we see him in the  modern day sometimes his ruthless intent  goes a bit out of whack  which makes me genuinely question  whether or not I should like him a lot  of the time and that is great I love  that Jonny is not a simple man and that  wid the right framing you could very  easily paint him as a villain but  speaking of villains this series does  have some strict antagonists one of  which is Dyna brando which right cool  wasn't expecting dinosaurs in a Western  but that's what makes Bizarre Adventure  so wonderfully bizarre it will say that  this is almost certainly my favorite  incarnation of deer because while he  retains his British nature to a fault he  also somehow is quite easy to root for  and I was always excited when he showed  up in a chapter as well as any other  alternate-dimension dealers and